1
00:00:00,370 --> 00:00:04,650
So, let's take an example. We will
return to these simple examples involving

2
00:00:04,650 --> 00:00:09,050
heights in a population, but this
time we will have two factors:

3
00:00:09,050 --> 00:00:14,230
sex, male or female, and nationality,
with several nationalities.

4
00:00:14,310 --> 00:00:19,510
We will generate data to see what
happens with and without an interaction

5
00:00:19,510 --> 00:00:24,770
and see how we can visualize this
concept of interaction graphically.

6
00:00:27,640 --> 00:00:33,460
So, in the first example, where
we would simply have main effects

7
00:00:33,460 --> 00:00:39,660
with an interaction term, we will
see that height is ultimately

8
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:45,300
a function of the mean height
of all the people measured,

9
00:00:46,040 --> 00:00:51,040
plus the effect of sex, plus
the effect of nationality,

10
00:00:51,140 --> 00:00:55,720
plus the effect of the interactions
between them, and of course,

11
00:00:55,720 --> 00:01:01,440
an error in our sample. There
will always be an error.

12
00:01:01,660 --> 00:01:04,880
People belonging to the same group and the

13
00:01:04,880 --> 00:01:07,760
same nationality do not all
have exactly the same height.

14
00:01:10,350 --> 00:01:13,890
So, in R, for example, we
can write this as an ANOVA

15
00:01:13,890 --> 00:01:18,330
where height is a function
of sex times nationality,

16
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:24,050
meaning the fixed effects of sex and
nationality, plus their interaction.

17
00:01:26,500 --> 00:01:30,140
In a first example, we
generated data that gave us

18
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:34,340
the following results. The
mean height of women of

19
00:01:34,340 --> 00:01:39,780
nationality A is 162, and for
nationality B it is 170: they are taller.

20
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,860
For men, it is 174 for nationality
A and 182 for nationality B.

21
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:50,700
If we plot these values on a graph
like this, we can connect the mean

22
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:57,040
heights of men with the orange line,
and of women with the blue line,

23
00:01:57,060 --> 00:02:01,180
and see that we obtain two parallel lines,

24
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:06,980
which indicates that the variation
due to nationality is the same,

25
00:02:12,900 --> 00:02:16,860
12 cm, whether we are looking
at nationality A or B.

26
00:02:24,070 --> 00:02:29,010
Here, in the example with an
interaction, we generated data such that

27
00:02:29,010 --> 00:02:36,150
the height difference is not the
same for nationality A as for

28
00:02:36,150 --> 00:02:39,990
nationality B between men and
women. So, when we plot the graphs,

29
00:02:39,990 --> 00:02:43,930
we see that our lines are no
longer parallel, indicating the

30
00:02:43,930 --> 00:02:50,150
presence of an interaction, which
we can interpret as height being

31
00:02:50,150 --> 00:02:57,310
a function of both nationality
and sex at the same time, and

32
00:02:57,310 --> 00:03:03,330
the height differences by
sex will not be the same

33
00:03:03,330 --> 00:03:05,470
depending on nationality, or vice versa.
